+/* Ask the application for the next chunk of its stream and wait for it.
+ *
+ * The application answers one message per turn of its own event loop, and a
+ * turn is only as short as the slowest thing in it: loading a level, or a
+ * browser that is slow to put a frame up, is a few hundred milliseconds during
+ * which nothing is answered. Waiting a whole second and then giving up was
+ * both too short for that and reported the wrong thing -- a return of 0 means
+ * "the stream is over", so one slow moment silenced the game for the rest of
+ * the session. An unanswered request now says so with -1, and waits long
+ * enough that abandoning one is a real failure rather than a busy moment:
+ * a reply that arrives after we stop listening lands in the queue as a stray,
+ * and fills the very buffer the next request is reading out of.
